{"name":"Faded Firebrick","hex":"#e5d9dc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"faded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That has lost some of its former vividness and colour.","That has lost some of its former strength or vigour."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of fade"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/faded"},{"term":"firebrick","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brick capable of withstanding high temperatures without deforming.","The X11 color name of RGB hexadecimal value #B22222."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/firebrick"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fire brick","blocks":[{"text":"A fire brick, firebrick, fireclay brick, or refractory brick is a block of ceramic material used in lining furnaces, kilns, fireboxes, and fireplaces. Made of primarily oxide materials like silica and alumina in varying ratios, these insulating materials are able to withstand extremely high temperatures, and have a low thermal conductivity for greater energy efficiency. Refractory bricks generally range from 25-45% alumina, and ~60% silica, with additional magnesium, calcium, potassium oxides."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Torpedo_car_refractory_bricks.JPG/330px-Torpedo_car_refractory_bricks.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_brick","wikidata":"Q1369000"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"faded":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/En-us-faded.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6f/En-us-faded.ogg/En-us-faded.ogg.mp3"}],"firebrick":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-firebrick.wav?utm_source=en.wiktiona